# Domain Actions: Inputs, Variants, Capabilities, and Presentation

An approval button needs a named operation, typed inputs, policy decisions,
and a defined result. This workbook builds those pieces from a domain and
checks them without a database. Continue with the live action workbook to
execute a plan and test row guards.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_verification!()
```

## 1) Read the action beside its write policy

The [shared fixture](support/action_fixture.exs) is a complete authored domain.
It declares writable fields beside their column types, an `open → approved`
transition, a `ready = true` precondition, and two capabilities. Read that
fixture before adapting this example to an application.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/action_fixture.exs", __DIR__))
domain = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.domain()
assert {:ok, normalized, _diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(domain)
assert normalized.writes.fields.state.updatable
domain.actions.approve
```

The action's `confirmation` and `audit` describe what a host should present and
record. Planning does not display a dialog or persist an audit event.

## 2) Plan from caller intent and trusted host context

The user selects the action and target. The host resolves the authenticated
actor. Execution templates may read `{:input, field}` or `{:context, field}`;
only the planner's `trusted_context` option supplies the latter.

```elixir
intent = %{
  action: :approve, target: %{id: "1"},
  context: %{actor_id: 999}, preconditions: []
}
assert {:ok, plan} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, intent,
  trusted_context: %{actor_id: 17})
assert plan.changes == %{state: "approved", reviewed_by_id: 17}
assert {"id", 1} in plan.filters
assert {"ready", true} in plan.filters
assert {"state", "open"} in plan.filters
assert plan.expected_cardinality == {:exactly, 1}
assert %SelectoUpdato.PlannedOperation{} = plan.operation_builder

assert {:error, %{code: :missing_action_context_reference}} =
  SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, intent)

Map.take(plan, [:action, :inputs, :filters, :changes, :preconditions, :confirmation])
```

The planned builder is opaque. It cannot be passed straight to raw write APIs.
The host execution adapter will receive a builder after authorization.

```elixir
runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only)
target = Selecto.configure(domain, runtime)
assert {:error, %{details: %{code: :opaque_planned_operation}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.preview(plan.operation_builder, target)
```

## 3) Choose variants after input coercion

The `record_review` action has an accepted path and a needs-work path. Only the
second path requires a reason. The planner checks the selected variant's inputs.

```elixir
assert {:ok, accepted} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, %{
  action: :record_review, target: 1, inputs: %{accepted: "true"}
})
assert accepted.inputs["accepted"] == true
assert accepted.changes == %{note: "Review accepted"}

assert {:error, _} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, %{
  action: :record_review, target: 1, inputs: %{accepted: "false"}
})
assert {:ok, needs_work} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, %{
  action: :record_review, target: 1,
  inputs: %{accepted: "false", reason: "Missing packing details"}
})
assert needs_work.changes == %{note: "Missing packing details"}
assert {:error, _} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain, %{
  action: :record_review, target: 1, inputs: %{accepted: "perhaps"}
})
```

## 4) Ask the host policy for each phase

This small policy allows approvers to preview, and requires a separate apply
permission for execution. In an application, both permission values come from
the authenticated host. A preview decision does not authorize a later apply.

```elixir
policy = fn request, context, _opts ->
  cond do
    request.capability != "approvals.approve" -> :hidden
    context.actor.role != :approver -> :hidden
    request.phase == :execute and not context.actor.can_apply ->
      %{status: :disabled, reason: "Apply permission is required"}
    true -> :enabled
  end
end
context = %{actor: %{id: 17, role: :approver, can_apply: false}, tenant: %{tenant_id: 101}}

assert {:error, {:validation_error, _, %{code: :missing_capability_resolver}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.authorize_domain_action(plan, :preview, context)
assert {:ok, preview_decision} =
  SelectoUpdato.authorize_domain_action(plan, :preview, context, capability_resolver: policy)
assert preview_decision.status == :enabled
assert {:error, {:validation_error, _, %{status: :disabled}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.authorize_domain_action(plan, :execute, context, capability_resolver: policy)
```

## 5) Drive action presentation from the contract

The generated document is suitable for Components' action helpers. These calls
produce presentation data; they do not render LiveView or authorize writes.

```elixir
assert {:ok, document, _diagnostics} = SelectoUpdato.DomainContract.json_document(domain)
items = SelectoComponents.Actions.available(document, decisions: %{
  "approvals.approve" => %{status: :disabled, reason: "Apply permission is required"},
  "approvals.review" => %{status: :hidden}
})
assert [item] = items
assert item.id == "approve"
assert item.disabled?
assert item.requires_confirmation?
assert item.reason == "Apply permission is required"
assert SelectoComponents.Actions.selection_fields(domain) == ["eligible"]

action = domain.actions.approve
assert SelectoComponents.Actions.row_eligible?(action, %{eligible: true})
refute SelectoComponents.Actions.row_eligible?(action, %{eligible: nil})
refute SelectoComponents.Actions.row_eligible?(action, %{})
items
```

## 6) Make bulk selection explicit

The domain enables bulk updates and this action opts into bulk selection.
Concrete selected IDs produce an exact expected count. Duplicate or empty
selections are rejected before execution.

```elixir
assert {:ok, bulk} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain,
  %{action: :approve, target: %{ids: ["1", "4"]}}, trusted_context: %{actor_id: 17})
assert bulk.scope == "bulk"
assert bulk.expected_cardinality == {:exactly, 2}
for ids <- [[], [1, 1]] do
  assert {:error, %{code: :invalid_action_target}} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain,
    %{action: :approve, target: %{ids: ids}}, trusted_context: %{actor_id: 17})
end
```

Continue with [guarded action execution](selecto_action_execution_workbook.livemd)
to test readiness changes, tenant isolation, and rollback on partial bulk matches.
